2, 2, d} = (\\textrm{Id}_1^{\\oplus 4d})\\oplus \\left( \\bigoplus _{a = 1}^{\\lfloor d/2\\rfloor }\\textsf{RSK}_{aa, aa}^{\\oplus 4(d-2a)+\\delta _{d, 2a}}\\right) . \\end{aligned}$$\n\nExample 5.7 shows that \\(\\textrm{Tr}\\ \\textsf{RSK}_{aa, aa}\\) is 0 for a odd and 1 for a even. This yields a trace formula matching the recurrence \\(s_d = 4d + s_{d-4}\\) for the concentric square numbers \\((s_d)\\):\n\n$$\\begin{aligned} \\textrm{Tr}\\ \\textsf{RSK}_{2, 2, d} = 4d+\\sum _{b=1}^{\\lfloor d/4\\rfloor }(4(d-4b)+\\delta _{d, 4b}). \\end{aligned}$$\n\nTheorem 8.1 shows that formulas for \\(\\textrm{Tr}\\ \\textsf{RSK}_{1^d, 1^d}\\) determine the growth of \\(\\textrm{Tr} \\ \\textsf{RSK}_{m,n,d}\\) except in the “accidental” cases (such as \\(d=2\\)) where \\(\\textrm{Tr}\\ \\textsf{RSK}_{1^d, 1^d}=0\\). We see\n\n$$\\begin{aligned} \\lim _{m, n\\rightarrow \\infty } \\textrm{Tr} \\ \\textsf{RSK}_{m,n,d}\\rightarrow -\\infty \\text { if }d=3,4 \\end{aligned}$$\n\nand yet\n\n$$\\begin{aligned} \\lim _{m, n\\rightarrow \\infty } \\textrm{Tr} \\ \\textsf{RSK}_{m,n,d}\\rightarrow \\infty \\text { if }d=5. \\end{aligned}$$\n\nWhat are the values (or even the signs) of \\(\\textrm{Tr}\\ \\textsf{RSK}_{1^d, 1^d}\\) for \\(d\\ge 1\\)? In particular, is \\(\\textrm{Tr}\\ \\textsf{RSK}_{1^d, 1^d}\\) always positive (or always negative) for d sufficiently large? To better investigate \\(\\textrm{Tr}\\ \\textsf{RSK}_{1^d, 1^d}\\), we record a practical formula that follows immediately from Proposition 4.1. It allowed us to compute up to \\(d = 11\\):\n\n$$\\begin{aligned} \\{\\textrm{Tr} \\ \\textsf{RSK}_{1^d,1^d}\\}_{d\\ge 1}= \\{1,0,-3,-5,23,96,-279,-3498,124, 120819, 185838, \\ldots \\}. \\end{aligned}$$\n\n### Corollary 8.4\n\n$$\\begin{aligned} \\textrm{Tr} \\ \\textsf{RSK}_{1^d,1^d}= \\sum _{\\alpha \\in \\textsf{Cont}_{1^d, 1^d}} \\prod _c \\det \\alpha _c(\\alpha ). \\end{aligned}$$\n\n### Conjecture 8.5\n\n\\(\\textrm{Tr}\\ \\textsf{RSK}_{1^d, 1^d}\\ne 0\\) for \\(d\\ne 2\\), i.e., \\(\\textrm{Tr} \\ \\textsf{RSK}_{m,n,d}\\) has total degree 2d for \\(d\\ne 2\\).\n\nIn order to compute \\(\\textrm{Tr}\\ \\textsf{RSK}_{m, n, d}\\) more quickly, one must identify more efficient formulas for computing the diagonal entries \\(\\textsf{RSK}_{\\sigma , \\pi }(\\alpha , \\alpha )\\). We have such formulas for each of the families from Sect. 4 (see Proposition 4.1, Proposition 4.3, and Proposition 5.6). In these cases we can compute \\(\\textsf{RSK}_{\\sigma , \\pi }(\\alpha , \\alpha )\\) without determining the entire matrix, and it turns out that \\(\\textsf{RSK}_{\\sigma , \\pi }(\\alpha , \\alpha )\\in \\{-1, 0, 1\\}\\). However, we know of no formula to compute individual entries \\(\\textsf{RSK}_{\\sigma , \\pi }(\\alpha , \\alpha )\\) for arbitrary weights \\((\\sigma , \\pi )\\), and our next result shows that these diagonal entries can be arbitrarily large.\n\n### Proposition 8.6\n\nFor all \\(N\\in {\\mathbb N}\\) there exist \\((\\sigma , \\pi )\\) with \\((\\ell (\\sigma ), \\ell (\\pi )) = (2,3)\\) and \\(\\alpha \\in \\textsf{Cont}_{\\sigma , \\pi }\\) such that \\(|\\textsf{RSK}_{\\sigma , \\pi }(\\alpha , \\alpha )| = N\\).\n\n### Proof\n\nWe provide an explicit construction of \\(\\sigma \\), \\(\\pi \\), and \\(\\alpha \\). Let\n\n$$\\begin{aligned} \\sigma = (N+2, N+1) \\text { and } \\pi = (N+1, N+1, 1). \\end{aligned}$$\n\nFurther, let \\(\\alpha = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & n & 1\\\\ n & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\in \\textsf{Cont}_{\\sigma , \\pi }\\). Then one can verify directly that\n\n$$\\begin{aligned} \\textsf{RSK}(z^\\alpha ) = \\begin{vmatrix} z_{11}&z_{12}\\\\ z_{21}&z_{22}\\end{vmatrix}^N\\begin{vmatrix}z_{11}&z_{13}\\\\ z_{21}&z_{23}\\end{vmatrix}z_{12}. \\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince \\(z_{11}z_{23}\\) is not a factor of \\(z^\\alpha \\), we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned} \\textsf{RSK}_{\\sigma , \\pi }(\\alpha , \\alpha )&= [z^\\alpha ]\\left( \\begin{vmatrix} z_{11}&z_{12}\\\\ z_{21}&z_{22}\\end{vmatrix}^N\\begin{vmatrix}z_{11}&z_{13}\\\\ z_{21}&z_{23}\\end{vmatrix}z_{12}\\right) \\\\&= [z_{11}z_{12}^{N-1}z_{21}^{N-1}z_{22}]\\left( \\begin{vmatrix}z_{11}&z_{12}\\\\ z_{21}&z_{22}\\end{vmatrix}^N\\right) \\cdot [z_{21}z_{13}z_{12}]\\left( \\begin{vmatrix}z_{11}&z_{13}\\\\ z_{21}&z_{23}\\end{vmatrix}z_{12}\\right) \\\\&= (-1)^{N-1}{N\\atopwithdelims ()1}\\cdot (-1)\\\\&= (-1)^NN. \\end{aligned}$$\n\n\\(\\square \\)\n\nEven for permutation weights, one may hope for a more efficient formula to determine when diagonal entries of \\(\\textsf{RSK}_{1^d, 1^d}\\) are 0. Let \\(C_d\\) denote the set of \\(\\alpha \\in \\textsf{Cont}_{1^d, 1^d}\\) such that the diagonal entry \\(\\textsf{RSK}_{1^d, 1^d}(\\alpha , \\alpha ) = 0\\). We computed that,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned} \\{|C_d|\\}_{d\\ge 1} = \\{0,0,1,7,53,406,3373,30360, 297256, 3153559, 36186708,\\dots \\}. \\end{aligned}$$\n\n### Conjecture 8.7\n\n\\(\\lim _{d\\rightarrow \\infty } |C_d|/d! = 1\\).\n\nBy Corollary 8.4, to prove Conjecture 8.7 it suffices to show the following. Under RSK insertion of almost any permutation matrix (i.e., Schensted insertion), some label in the first row of the partially completed P tableau gets bumped to a column strictly to the left.^{Footnote 7}\n\nSince \\(\\textsf{RSK}_{\\sigma , \\pi }\\) and \\(\\textsf{RSK}_{\\widetilde{\\sigma }, \\widetilde{\\pi }}\\) are similar if and only if their inverses are, a result analogous to Theorem 3.22 holds for the matrices \\(\\textsf{RSK}^{-1}_{m, n, d}\\). This implies similar results on the trace, determinant, and so on. We provide trace data for \\(\\textsf{RSK}^{-1}_{m, m, d}\\) for the interested reader (Table 4).\n\nTable 4 Values of \\(\\textrm{Tr} \\ \\textsf{RSK}_{m,m,d}^{-1}\\)\n\nNote that \\(\\textrm{Tr}\\ \\textsf{RSK}^{-1}_{2, 2, d} = \\textrm{Tr}\\ \\textsf{RSK}_{2,2, d}\\); this is because all of the reduced pairs \\((\\sigma , \\pi )\\) contributing to the trace in this special case of Theorem 8.1 are triangular and therefore self-inverse by the calculation in the proof of Proposition 6.1.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRSK as a linear operator (https://www2.math.ethz.ch/EMIS/journals/SLC/wpapers/FPSAC2025/24.pdf)\nciteturn4search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.3 years ago; RSK as a linear operator                                                                 11 ... *Proof Sketch. ... ## 4.3  Trace of \\(\\mathrm{RSK}_{m,n,d}\\) ... To investigate Conjecture 4.6, we record a practical formula that follows from Proposition 3.1.\nRSK as a linear operator                                                                 11\n\n**Theorem 4.2.** Let \\(m,n,d \\in \\mathbb{N}\\).\n\n(I) Fix \\(d\\) and let \\(r\\) be the least positive integer such that \\(2^r > d\\). Then \\(\\det \\mathrm{RSK}_{m,n,d}\\) has period \\(2^r\\) in both \\(m\\) and \\(n\\), i.e., \\(\\det \\mathrm{RSK}_{m,n,d} = \\det \\mathrm{RSK}_{m+2^r,n,d} = \\det \\mathrm{RSK}_{m,n+2^r,d}\\).\n\n(II) In the case where \\(m=n\\) we have the formula \\(\\det \\mathrm{RSK}_{m,n,d} = \\prod_{\\sigma} \\det \\mathrm{RSK}_{\\sigma,\\sigma}\\). The product is over reduced pairs \\((\\sigma,\\sigma)\\) of degree \\(d' \\le d\\) such that \\(A_{\\sigma,\\sigma}(d)\\binom{m}{\\ell(\\sigma)}\\) is odd.\n\n*Proof Sketch.* Both (I) and (II) are direct consequences of Theorem 1.9 and the parity of the constants \\(N_{\\sigma,\\pi}(m,n,d)\\). Part (I) follows from Lucas’s theorem on the parity of \\(\\binom{a}{b}\\), while part (II) follows from the fact that \\(N_{\\sigma,\\pi}(m,m,d)=2A_{\\sigma,\\pi}(d)\\binom{m}{\\ell(\\sigma)}\\binom{m}{\\ell(\\pi)}\\) when \\(\\sigma \\ne \\pi\\). □\n\n*Example 4.3.* By Theorem 4.2, \\(\\det \\mathrm{RSK}_{2^k,2^k,d}=1\\) for all \\(d<2^k\\). Indeed, by Lucas’s theorem, each \\(N_{\\sigma,\\sigma}(2^k,2^k,d)\\) for reduced \\((\\sigma,\\sigma)\\) is even, since \\(\\ell(\\sigma)\\le d<2^k\\).\n\n*Remark 4.4.* For a fixed \\(d\\), the period of \\(\\det \\mathrm{RSK}_{m,n,d}\\) given in Theorem 4.2(I) need not be minimal. For instance, the minimal period of \\(\\det \\mathrm{RSK}_{m,n,4}\\) is 4 rather than 8.\n\nFor any fixed \\(d\\), Theorem 4.2(II) allows one to in principle determine \\(\\det \\mathrm{RSK}_{m,m,d}\\) by computing \\(\\det \\mathrm{RSK}_{\\sigma,\\sigma}\\) for a finite collection of weights \\(\\sigma\\).\n\n## 4.3  Trace of \\(\\mathrm{RSK}_{m,n,d}\\)\n\nThe first sentence of Theorem 4.5 below is Theorem 1.1(IV).\n\n**Theorem 4.5.** *For fixed \\(d\\), \\(\\mathrm{Tr}\\,\\mathrm{RSK}_{m,n,d}\\) is a polynomial in \\(O(m^dn^d)\\). More specifically,*\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{Tr}\\,\\mathrm{RSK}_{m,n,d}\n=\nN_0(m,n,d)\n+\n\\sum_{(\\sigma,\\pi)} N_{\\sigma,\\pi}(m,n,d)\\,\\mathrm{Tr}\\,\\mathrm{RSK}_{\\sigma,\\pi}.\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nThe sum is over nonzero reduced pairs \\((\\sigma,\\pi)\\) of degree \\(d' \\le d\\), and \\(N_{\\sigma,\\pi}(m,n,d)\\) and \\(N_0(m,n,d)\\) are as in Theorem 1.9. The lead term is \\(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{Tr}\\,\\mathrm{RSK}_{1^d,1^d}}{(d!)^2} m^d n^d\\) whenever \\(\\mathrm{Tr}\\,\\mathrm{RSK}_{1^d,1^d} \\ne 0\\).\n\n*Proof.* The first formula is immediate from Theorem 1.9. The formula for \\(N_{\\sigma,\\pi}(m,n,d)\\) in Theorem 1.9 shows that for fixed \\(d\\), \\(N_{\\sigma,\\pi}(m,n,d)\\) is a polynomial in \\(m\\) and \\(n\\) of total degree \\(\\ell(\\sigma)+\\ell(\\pi)\\). Similarly, the expression for \\(N_0(m,n,d)\\) is a polynomial of total degree \\(d+1\\). Hence \\(\\mathrm{Tr}\\,\\mathrm{RSK}_{m,n,d}\\) is polynomial for any fixed \\(d\\). Moreover, the fastest-growing summand in (4.1) is \\(N_{1^d,1^d}(m,n,d)\\), which has lead term \\(\\dfrac{(mn)^d}{(d!)^2}\\). □\n\n**Conjecture 4.6.** \\(\\mathrm{Tr}\\,\\mathrm{RSK}_{1^d,1^d} \\ne 0\\) for \\(d \\ne 2\\), i.e., \\(\\mathrm{Tr}\\,\\mathrm{RSK}_{m,n,d}\\) has total degree \\(2d\\) for \\(d \\ne 2\\).\n\nTo investigate Conjecture 4.6, we record a practical formula that follows from Proposition 3.1. It allowed us to compute up to \\(d=11\\):\n\n\\[\n\\{\\mathrm{Tr}\\,\\mathrm{RSK}_{1^d,1^d}\\}_{d\\ge 1}\n=\n\\{1,0,-3,-5,23,96,-279,-3498,124,120819,185838,\\ldots\\}.\n\\]\n\n**Corollary 4.7.** \\(\\mathrm{Tr}\\,\\mathrm{RSK}_{1^d,1^d} = \\sum_{\\alpha \\in \\mathrm{Cont}_{1^d,1^d}} \\prod_c \\det \\alpha_c(\\alpha).\\)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSéminaire Lotharingien de Combinatoire 93B (2025) (https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~slc/wpapers/FPSAC2025/24.pdf)\nciteturn4search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; RSK as a linear operator ... (IV) (Theorem 4.5) For fixed d, the trace of RSKm,n,d is a polynomial in O(mdnd).\nRSK as a linear operator\n3\nTheorem 1.1. Let m, n, d ∈N.\n(I) (Section 4.1) The matrix RSKm,n,d is diagonalizable if and only if d ≤3, (m, n, d) ∈\n{(2, 3, 6), (3, 2, 6)}, or\n1\nm + 1\nn + 1\nd > 1.\n(II) (Section 4.1) The characteristic polynomial of RSKm,n,d is not solvable by radicals whenever\nm, n ≥3 and d ≥4.\n(III) (Theorem 4.2) Fix d and let r be minimal such that 2r > d. The function det RSKm,n,d has\nperiod 2r in both m and n, i.e.,\ndet RSKm,n,d = det RSKm+2r,n,d = det RSKm,n+2r,d.\n(IV) (Theorem 4.5) For fixed d, the trace of RSKm,n,d is a polynomial in O(mdnd).\nTheorem 1.1(I) can be rephrased using Dynkin diagrams. Let Gm,n,d be the graph\nconsisting of three paths of lengths m, n and d adjoined at one node in a “⊥” shape (so\n|Gm,n,d| = m + n + d −2). Theorem 1.1(I) states that RSKm,n,d is diagonalizable if and only\nif d ≤3 or Gm,n,d is a Dynkin diagram of type Ak, Dk, E6, E7, E8, or E9 (see Figure 1).\nE6\nE7\nE8\nE9\nAk\nDk\nFigure 1: The Dynkin diagrams corresponding to diagonalizable matrices RSKm,n,d.\nOur proofs use Theorem 1.9, which concerns the further restriction of RSK to weight\nspaces described below. The weight space arguments also yield formulas for the deter-\nminant and trace of RSKm,n,d that are more efficient than the naïve algorithms.\n1.2\nWeight spaces and RSK-commuting maps\nA pair (σ, π) ∈Nm × Nn has degree d if d = |σ| = |π|, where |σ| := ∑m\ni=1 σi. The weight\nspace Rm,n,σ,π ⊆Rm,n,d is the subspace spanned by degree-d monomials zα such that\n∑\nj\nαi,j = σi, 1 ≤i ≤m and ∑\ni\nαi,j = πj, 1 ≤j ≤n.\n(1.1)\nEquivalently, α is a contingency table with row margins σ and column margins π. Now,\nRm,n,d =\nM\nσ,π:|σ|=|π|=d\nRm,n,σ,π.\n(1.2)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRSK as a linear operator (https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.23009)\nciteturn4academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.0 years ago; Title: RSK as a linear operator ... We study it as a linear operator on the coordinate ring of matrices, proving results about its diagonalizability, eigenvalues, trace, and determinant.\nTitle: RSK as a linear operator\nAuthors: Ada Stelzer, Alexander Yong\nDate: Wed Oct 30 13:35:30 2024\n\nThe Robinson-Schensted-Knuth correspondence (RSK) is a bijection between nonnegative integer matrices and pairs of Young tableaux. We study it as a linear operator on the coordinate ring of matrices, proving results about its diagonalizability, eigenvalues, trace, and determinant. Our criterion for diagonalizability involves the $ADE$ classification of Dynkin diagrams, as well as the diagram for $E_9$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCops and attacking robbers with cycle constraints - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0166218X25002665)\nciteturn4search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; This paper considers the Cops and Attacking Robbers game, a variant of Cops and Robbers, where the robber is empowered to attack a cop in the same way a cop can capture the robber. ... We give a sufficient condition for a triangle-free graph to have attacking cop number at most 2 and we characterise when outerplanar graphs have attacking cop number 2. ... Finally we construct 17 non-isomorphic graphs H of order 58 with c